The five things I'll remember

1. Generative AI creates, where traditional AI predicts. Old machine learning tells you an email is spam. Generative AI writes the reply. You go from getting a label to getting a piece of work.

2. Set temperature or top-P, never both. They do the same job. Change both and you can't reproduce your own results. Reading invoices? Temperature 0. Writing marketing copy? Turn it up.

3. A model knows nothing about your business until you show it. RAG fixes that by searching your documents first and answering from what it finds. And when a policy changes you update the document — you don't retrain anything.

4. Chunking matters more than I expected. Cut a document in the wrong place and the model sees "employees may carry over holiday" without "up to a maximum of five days". The answer is fluent, confident and wrong. Easy to create, hard to spot.

5. A carefully worded instruction is not security. Your instruction sits inside the model's thinking, and that's exactly what a prompt injection targets. A guardrail runs outside the model. Better still, strip personal data out before you store it — you can't leak what was never there.


